<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#union-flag">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Union Flag</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Union Jack</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Union Flag, or Union Jack, is the national flag of the United Kingdom. Its present form dates from 1 January 1801, when the red saltire of St Patrick, representing Ireland, was added to the crosses of St George for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and St Andrew for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, which had been combined on the flag since 1606.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/union-flag.jpg" alt="Union Flag" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(1-2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The new state’s flag, the <b>Union Flag</b>, added the cross of St Patrick to those of St George and St Andrew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Britain’s national symbols were made before the English word nationalism appeared, around 1798: John Arbuthnot invented John Bull in 1712, Thomas Arne composed “Rule, Britannia!” in 1740, and the <b>Union Jack</b> was adopted as the national flag in 1801.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…December 1961 in the new national stadium in Dar es Salaam, before 75,000 people, as Tanganyika's independence from the British Empire approached: near midnight the <b>Union Jack</b> was lowered to &quot;God Save the Queen&quot; for the last time, and the new flag was raised to the hymn's tune.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a></li><li><blockquote>In Philadelphia, Lieutenant John Paul Jones hoisted the Continental <b>Union Flag</b> aboard the Alfred, a frigate of the Continental Navy, the first vessel to fly the flag.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-3">3 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the siege of Boston, the Continental Army hoisted the Grand <b>Union Flag</b> on Prospect Hill near Cambridge, Massachusetts.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
